@brief
The last job in the dojo. Nothing in it is new. All of it is Tuesday.

    ORD-1500  Support need to put an order on hold while they talk to
              the customer, instead of cancelling it and taking the
              order again afterwards.

              Cut-off for the release is 17:00 and it is 16:20. The
              branch is finished apart from the provider and the
              resolver, and `git merge main` has just failed.

Four things, in this order.

  **1. The merge.** Two files conflicted.

  `order.entity.ts` — `main` added `'REFUNDED'` to the status union and
  you added `'ON_HOLD'`. Both sides are one line, and the answer is one
  line with both members on it, `'REFUNDED'` first.

  `registry.ts` — two hunks, both of them "each side added a
  registration and both are wanted". No spacing to preserve.

  **2. The error code.** `ORDER_ON_HOLD`, a 409, not reported, directly
  under `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`.

  **3. The feature.**

  `order.provider.ts` — a `hold(id: string, reason: string)` below
  `cancel`, one blank line between them. Same lookup, same shipped
  guard, and an update with
  `values: { status: 'ON_HOLD', holdReason: reason }`.

  `order.resolver.ts` — `holdOrder` below `cancelOrder`, taking
  `args: { id: string; reason: string }` and calling
  `this.orderProvider.hold(args.id, args.reason)`.

  **4. The review comment.** There is a commented-out spike at the top
  of the provider that a reviewer asked you to delete three weeks ago.
  It is the only comment in the file. It goes.

`docs/statuses.md` is documentation and is somebody else's ticket. The
migration under prisma/ is immutable. Neither changes.

Par is 402 keystrokes. Every new line in this ticket already exists
somewhere, in a form that needs two or three words changed.

@keys
| `$` `i` | append just before the last character of the line |
| `3dd` | three lines |
| `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` | sweep every conflict marker in the buffer |
| `:g/^\s*\/\//d` | delete every comment line |
| `13Y` `12j` `p` | copy a method with its blank line, put it after itself |
| `ciw` `ci{` `ci"` | change the word, the braces, the string |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer, write everything |

@hints
Merge first, always. Finishing a feature on top of an unresolved merge
means doing the interesting work twice when the resolution turns out to
disagree with it.
--
The entity conflict is a one-line merge, so it is not a delete-a-block
job. Delete the `<<<<<<<` line, then append your member to the end of
`main`'s line before the semicolon, then take the remaining three lines
in one `3dd`.
--
The registry has two hunks and both are the easy kind, so one
`:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` does the whole file. That command is only safe when
every hunk in the buffer is "keep both" — here it is; in the entity it
was not.
--
For the provider: `/public async cancel` `Enter` `k` `13Y` `12j` `p`
gives you a correctly spaced copy of `cancel` to turn into `hold`. Then
edit the copy by searching forwards — everything you need to change
occurs once above you and once in the copy, so `/status: 'CANCELLED'`
`Enter` goes to the one you mean.

@solution
A route that measures 349 keystrokes.

**entity** `/^<<<` `Enter` `dd` `$` `i | 'ON_HOLD'` `Esc` `j` `3dd`

**registry** `:bn` `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d`

**errors** `:bn` `/ALREADY_SHIPPED` `Enter` `yyp` `ci"ORDER_ON_HOLD` `Esc`

**provider** `:bn` `:g/^\s*\/\//d`, then `/public async cancel` `Enter`
`k` `13Y` `12j` `p`, then on the copy: `j` `www` `ciwhold` `Esc`,
`f)` `i, reason: string` `Esc`, `/status: 'CANCELLED'` `Enter`
`ci{ status: 'ON_HOLD', holdReason: reason ` `Esc`

**resolver** `:bn` `/cancelOrder` `Enter` `k` `4Y` `3j` `p`, then `j`
`www` `ciwholdOrder` `Esc`, `f{` `ci{ id: string; reason: string ` `Esc`,
`/cancel(args` `Enter` `ciwhold` `Esc` `f)` `i, args.reason` `Esc`

then `:wqa`.

Four things are worth saying at the end of a year.

**Not every conflict is the same conflict.** Three of the four hunks here
are "both sides added a thing" and fall to one sweep command. The fourth
is two versions of the same line and needs a merge by hand, in this case
an `$` and an `i`. Running the sweep on the entity would have left you
with two definitions of `OrderStatus`, the second shadowing the first,
and TypeScript would have told you — eventually. Look at each hunk and
ask what shape it is before you reach for the command that fixes the
shape you saw last.

**Order the work by what blocks what.** Merge, then error code, then the
layers in dependency order, then the tidy-up. Not because it is neater,
but because each step tells you the name the next one needs:
`'ON_HOLD'` comes out of the merge, `ORDER_ON_HOLD` out of the registry
pass, `hold` out of the provider. Working in that order means you never
go back to check a spelling.

**Copy the neighbour, every time.** The provider method, the resolver
method and the JSON line in this ticket are 90% identical to something
four lines away, and copying brings the house style with it for free.
This is the single habit that separates a fast afternoon from a slow one,
and it has nothing to do with typing speed.

**And the keystrokes were never the point.** You have spent a year on
motions and operators and registers and macros, and the reason none of
this ticket needed a new key is that the vocabulary ran out three
chapters ago. What you have been building since then is the reflex of
looking at a change, naming its shape — many, lines, a few, order,
structure, position — and reaching for the tool that fits it without
having to think. That reflex is the whole of the skill. Everything else
is practice, and practice is a Tuesday with a ticket on it.

